# Friends Like These Architecture

## 1. Purpose

This document describes the intended architecture of the rebuilt Friends Like These application.

Friends Like These is a live games-night scoring and event-management system.

The application must support multiple devices simultaneously, including:

- TVs
- Desktop computers
- Laptops
- Tablets
- Mobile phones

The architecture must therefore separate the user interface from persistent application state and business logic.

---

# 2. High-Level Architecture

The target architecture is:

```text
                 USERS
                   |
        -------------------------
        |           |           |
        v           v           v
       TV         Laptop      Mobile
        |           |           |
        -------- React ----------
                   |
                   |
                REST API
                   |
                   v
              Spring Boot
                   |
                   |
                  JPA
                   |
                   v
              PostgreSQL
```

The React frontend is responsible for presentation and interaction.

Spring Boot is responsible for application logic, security, validation and persistence operations.

PostgreSQL is responsible for persistent data storage.

---

# 3. Frontend Responsibilities

The frontend is responsible for:

- Rendering the public scoreboard
- Rendering the admin dashboard
- Responsive layouts
- User interaction
- Form input
- Displaying validation feedback
- Calling backend APIs
- Displaying backend data
- Receiving future real-time score updates

The frontend should not ultimately be responsible for permanently storing important event data.

---

# 4. Backend Responsibilities

The backend will become the authoritative source for:

- Teams
- Team members
- Scores
- Score events
- Event settings
- Registration deadlines
- Users
- Roles
- Authentication
- Authorisation

The backend will also enforce business rules.

For example:

```text
Admin awards points
        |
        v
Controller receives request
        |
        v
Service validates operation
        |
        v
Team score changes
        |
        +----> ScoreEvent created
        |
        v
Database transaction committed
```

---

# 5. Backend Design Approach

The backend will use object-oriented programming with a lightweight domain-oriented architecture.

The project will not initially implement full enterprise Domain-Driven Design.

Instead, useful DDD principles will be applied where they improve clarity.

These include:

- Clear domain models
- Business logic separated from controllers
- Explicit application services
- Repository abstraction through Spring Data
- DTOs at API boundaries
- Domain-specific naming
- Clearly defined responsibilities

---

# 6. Planned Backend Packages

The backend will use a structure similar to:

```text
com.friendslikethese.backend
|
|-- controller
|
|-- service
|
|-- repository
|
|-- domain
|
|-- dto
|
|-- security
|
|-- config
|
`-- exception
```

---

# 7. Controller Layer

Controllers form the HTTP boundary of the backend.

Their responsibilities include:

- Receiving HTTP requests
- Reading request parameters
- Reading request bodies
- Invoking services
- Returning HTTP responses

Controllers should remain thin.

Business logic should not be placed directly inside controllers.

Example:

```text
POST /api/teams
       |
       v
TeamController
       |
       v
TeamService
```

---

# 8. Service Layer

Services contain the application's main business operations.

Examples include:

- Creating a team
- Updating a team
- Deleting a team
- Awarding points
- Deducting points
- Resetting scores
- Recording score events

The service layer provides separation between HTTP communication and domain operations.

---

# 9. Repository Layer

Repositories provide database access.

Spring Data JPA will be used to reduce repetitive persistence code.

Example:

```text
TeamService
     |
     v
TeamRepository
     |
     v
PostgreSQL
```

Repositories should focus on persistence rather than UI or HTTP concerns.

---

# 10. Domain Layer

The domain layer represents important application concepts.

Initial domain concepts are expected to include:

```text
Team
ScoreEvent
EventSettings
User
Role
```

These may evolve as backend development progresses.

---

# 11. DTO Layer

DTO means Data Transfer Object.

DTOs will be used to control information crossing the API boundary.

This prevents the API from becoming unnecessarily coupled directly to database entities.

Possible DTOs include:

```text
CreateTeamRequest
UpdateTeamRequest
TeamResponse
ScoreAdjustmentRequest
LoginRequest
LoginResponse
```

---

# 12. Team Domain

A team currently contains:

```text
id
name
members
score
```

The backend representation may evolve depending on how team members are persisted.

Important rules include:

- A team must have a valid name.
- Team identifiers must be unique.
- Scores must not become negative.
- Team updates must preserve data integrity.

---

# 13. Scoring Domain

Scoring is one of the central business operations in Friends Like These.

Current quick-score operations include:

```text
+5
+10
+15
+20
-5
Custom
```

A score adjustment should eventually perform two operations within one backend transaction:

```text
Update Team score

AND

Create ScoreEvent
```

This prevents the score and score history from becoming inconsistent.

---

# 14. Score Events

A score event records why a team's score changed.

The current frontend model contains:

```text
id
teamId
amount
reason
timestamp
```

The persistent backend model may contain additional audit information later.

Potential additions include:

```text
performedBy
eventType
previousScore
newScore
```

These are future design considerations and are not yet implemented.

---

# 15. Event Settings

Event settings currently include the registration deadline.

Future settings may include:

- Event name
- Registration status
- Event start time
- Event end time
- Registration URL
- Scoring configuration

These should eventually be stored in the database rather than hard-coded into React.

---

# 16. Authentication

Spring Security will provide backend security.

JWT authentication is planned.

The expected flow is:

```text
Admin
  |
  | username/password
  v
POST /api/auth/login
  |
  v
Spring Security
  |
  v
JWT issued
  |
  v
React stores authentication state
  |
  v
Protected API request
```

The exact token-storage strategy will be decided during security implementation.

---

# 17. Authorisation

Public users should be able to access public scoreboard information.

Administrative operations must be protected.

For example:

```text
PUBLIC

GET /api/teams
GET public scoreboard data


ADMIN

POST /api/teams
PUT /api/teams/{id}
DELETE /api/teams/{id}
POST score adjustments
PUT event settings
POST reset scores
```

The final permission model will be defined during backend security development.

---

# 18. Real-Time Communication

A core requirement is synchronisation between devices.

Example:

```text
Administrator phone
       |
       | +10
       v
Spring Boot
       |
       | update
       v
PostgreSQL
       |
       | live notification
       v
TV Scoreboard
```

The TV should not require a manual refresh after every score change.

The exact real-time technology will be selected during that development phase.

Possible approaches include:

- Server-Sent Events
- WebSockets

The choice has not yet been finalised.

---

# 19. Responsive Architecture

Responsive behaviour remains a frontend responsibility.

The backend does not need separate APIs for TVs, tablets and phones simply because their screen sizes differ.

All devices consume the same application data.

For example:

```text
GET /api/teams
```

may be consumed by:

```text
TV
Laptop
Tablet
Phone
```

React and CSS determine how that data is presented on each device.

---

# 20. Database

PostgreSQL is planned as the relational database.

Initial persistent concepts are expected to include:

```text
teams
score_events
event_settings
users
roles
```

The final schema will be designed alongside the backend domain rather than being created independently from application requirements.

---

# 21. Cloud Architecture

Azure is the planned backend cloud platform.

The intended direction is:

```text
React Frontend
      |
      v
Azure-hosted Spring Boot API
      |
      v
Azure Database for PostgreSQL
```

Azure App Service is currently the intended hosting option for Spring Boot.

Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server is the intended managed database direction.

These services will be configured later in the project.

---

# 22. Development Architecture

During local development:

```text
React
localhost:5173

        |
        v

Spring Boot
localhost:8080

        |
        v

PostgreSQL
localhost:5432
```

CORS configuration will allow the React development application to communicate with the Spring Boot API.

---

# 23. Error Handling

The backend should eventually use centralised exception handling.

Examples include:

```text
TeamNotFoundException
DuplicateTeamException
InvalidScoreAdjustmentException
```

These can be converted into appropriate HTTP responses by a global exception handler.

This keeps error handling consistent across controllers.

---

# 24. Validation

Backend validation must not depend entirely on React.

Even when the frontend validates user input, the backend must independently validate incoming data.

This protects the application when requests originate from:

- React
- API clients
- automated tools
- malformed requests

Jakarta Validation will be used where appropriate.

---

# 25. Architectural Principle

The central architectural rule for the rebuild is:

```text
The frontend displays state.

The backend controls persistent application state.

The database stores persistent state.
```

This separation allows Friends Like These to operate reliably across multiple devices during a live event.
